  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nA somewhat \"cooler\" day is progressing across much of the Mid-\nSouth compared to the past several days as a frontal boundary\nslowly traverses the area. The frontal boundary is currently\nsouth of the I-40 corridor, generally along the TN/MS stateline.\nLatest visible satellite imagery shows some additional cloudiness\nthrough this region, with some light echoes of KNQA. Since the\nfrontal boundary has yet to clear the area, our southern tier of\ncounties across north to central Mississippi are already seeing\nheat indices approach 100. A Heat Advisory will remain in effect\nfor these locations through the afternoon and early evening,\nwhere values may exceed 105. The aforementioned frontal boundary\nis expected to stall across portions of NE MS by this evening,\nwith some scattered showers lingering in the forecast tonight and\ninto Thursday. The good news for the entire area is that\ntemperatures will be closer to normal Thursday and Friday, with\nhigh temperatures in the 80s to low 90s. By Friday, with the\nnorthwest flow regime ongoing, a series of shortwaves will dive\ntowards the area and bring back scattered thunderstorm chances\nboth Friday and Saturday. At the same time, the aforementioned\nfrontal boundary will slowly lift back north as a warm front.\nThere is still some question as to how these systems will\ninteract, but will keep isolated to scattered PoPs in the\nforecast through much of Friday and Saturday. Some CAMs have\nstarted to come into range, but there is still significant\nuncertainty with timing of these systems. Given the time of year,\nwill not rule out a strong thunderstorm or two though this\nperiod, but there are currently too many unknowns to highlight\nany severe weather threat at this time.\n\nBy Sunday, high pressure will build to the west, keeping subtle\nnortherly to northwesterly flow through the area. As the high\npressure builds through the region into next week, this will\nallow for oppressive heat and humidity to return to the Mid-South\nwith generally dry conditions. Heat headlines may be needed as\nearly as Saturday across portions of the Delta, expanding across\nmuch of the Mid-South by Sunday and into early next week. Both\nthe 6 to 10 and 8 to 14 day temperature outlooks favor above\nnormal temperatures, so the oppressive heat will likely continue\ninto early August.",
